   "k": "j6k4di",
   "en": "Strategies and guides"
  },
  {
   "k": "1345498",
   "en": "The below is ⟦0⟧ player suggested strategies for ⟦1⟧. Bear in mind, due to the dynamic nature of the game, it may unfold differently for other players.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "l7sok4",
   "en": "Austria is an extremely difficult nation to play. The Germans are on your border, and will attempt to annex you. You have until around 1937-1938 to prepare. Focus on the industry branch of the focus tree, getting as many military factories as possible. You should then focus on the political branch. Going democratic or communist is ideal, so you can join a major faction when the Germans invade. Remaining fascist can get you manpower though, if you decide to remain fascist, ally Italy if possible, or Japan, though allying with Japan is less rewarding. You should make a lot of weaker infantry divisions, instead of trying to get ideal stats. This tends to make the AI hesitant to attack your lines. When the Germans attack you will mainly want to hold out in the mountains. If you are not playing iron man, you should save before the Germans press for Anschluss, because if the Germans make it through the mountains, they will have a stronger attack. Once you make your defensive line, you can just wait until the German AI overwhelms itself. Joining the Allies or Comintern will speed this up. Once you defeat the Germans, your army should be big enough to establish dominance in central Europe."
  },
  {
   "k": "22wcyn",
   "en": "The Anschluss Risk can be mitigated by finishing the focus \"Reform the Central Powers\", which causes Germany to be unable to do the \"Anschluss\" focus. It is possible to immediately dissolve the Central Powers faction upon finishing the focus and create a new faction with Italy. This will still cause Bulgaria and Turkey to join the faction, thereby creating a decently sized mediterran faction."
  },
